<!-- delrt: <del> measured against the REAL Runner.

     Every case below was rendered in the genuine ADRIFT 5.0.36 Runner
     (run500.exe under Wine) on 2026-08-11, and the expected transcript records
     what it printed, so this probe is ground truth rather than a guess:

       1  abc<del>            to  ab      plain backspace
       2  a<b>x</b><del>      to  a       deletes ACROSS a style boundary
       3  a<br><del>          to  a       deletes a <br>
       4  a\n<del>            to  a       deletes a source newline
       5  a\n\n<del>          to  a\n     only ONE newline goes
       6  the credits shape Larry Horsfield's games use,
          "websites:\n\n<del>https://..\n \n<del>http://..": the Runner keeps
          the stray leading space before the second URL
       7  a room LongDescription that STARTS with <del> joins onto the room
          name line
       8  ("eight") a <del> at the head of a LATER message reaches back into
          output already emitted this turn: the Runner deletes the two pSpace
          join spaces AND the last character of the previous message.

     Cases 9 to 11 (measured 2026-08-11) settle what pSpace does when the
     FIRST message of a multi-message turn ends in a tag rather than a bare
     newline.  Global.pSpace tests sText.EndsWith(vbLf) on the RAW accumulated
     text, tags included, so a message whose last source character is the '>'
     of a tag gets the two join spaces even when the character the player SEES
     last is a newline:

       9  ("nine")   msg A "9[x\n\n<del>" + msg B "]END9":   the <del> eats one
          newline, one newline SURVIVES, yet the join spaces are added, so
          the Runner prints "9[x\n  ]END9" (two-space indent on the new line).
          This is the case that killed the "sb_pspace should walk back over
          zero-width sentinels" hunch: the A5_ALR_MARK the del arm leaves as
          the buffer's final byte makes sb_pspace's final-byte test say
          "not a newline", which is exactly what EndsWith(vbLf) says about the
          raw "...<del>": the mask IS the runner's behaviour.
       10 ("ten")    msg A "10[a<br>" + msg B:  a trailing <br> ALSO gets the
          join spaces ("10[a\n  ]END10"), because the raw text ends '>', while
          the rendered text ends '\n'.
       11 ("eleven") msg A "11[a\n" (bare source newline) + msg B: control,
          no join spaces, "11[a\n]END11".

     Cases 1 to 7 live in the Introduction and the room description, so they
     render at load with no commands needed.  Case 8 needs one command
     ("eight"); it is the case that drove the turn-level A5_DEL_MARK /
     sb_resolve_del pass (a5sb.cpp), and the expected transcript now records
     the Runner's output for all eleven. -->
